      "text": "\n\nHere's what I see and what I'd propose:\n\n---\n\n**Current State**\n\nRight now the mountain cards are a compact list-row style — small icon on the left, name + location in the center, elevation on the right, and a stamp-style \"SUMMITED\" badge when applicable. The icons are individually generated PNG images stored as URLs in `mountainIcons.ts` (~100+ unique icons), and there's also a `silhouettes.ts` with SVG path data for each mountain that doesn't seem to be used on the cards currently.\n\n---\n\n**What I'd Do with the Cards**\n\nTwo main directions to consider:\n\n**Option A — Enhanced List Cards (keep the compact feel)**\n- Add a subtle background image/gradient strip to each card using the mountain's silhouette path data (from `silhouettes.ts`). This would render as a faint, atmospheric outline behind the text — giving each card a unique visual identity without making them huge.\n- Slightly increase vertical padding and make the icon larger (~44px instead of 34px) so the generated mountain images actually read well.\n- For summited mountains, keep the stamp badge as-is (you liked that treatment), but add a subtle green or gold left-border accent to make summited cards instantly scannable while scrolling.\n\n**Option B — Photo-style Cards (bigger, richer)**\n- Switch to a taller card format (~120-140px) with the mountain icon/image as a larger background element, overlaid with a dark gradient at the bottom where the name, location, and elevation sit.\n- This would feel more like an Airbnb listing or an Instagram-style feed — more visually engaging, but you'd see fewer peaks on screen at once.\n- The summit stamp would overlay the card, which could look dramatic.\n\n**My recommendation:** Option A. Your app has 100+ mountains, so a compact list that still feels distinct per-mountain is more practical. The silhouette data you already have is an untapped goldmine — rendering those as subtle background shapes would make each card feel custom without bloating the layout.\n\n---\n\n**For Mountain Imagery**\n\n- The individual generated PNGs in `mountainIcons.ts` are solid for the small icon role. I'd keep those as the primary card thumbnail.\n- On the **detail page** (`mountain/[id].tsx`), the silhouette SVG paths could be rendered larger as a hero element — a stylized mountain profile with snow/shadow layers using the multi-path data (`basePath`, `shadowPath`, `snowPath`, `ridgePath`). This would be unique and lightweight since it's all vector data.\n- For mountains that don't have a custom icon (custom/user-added peaks), I'd fall back to a category-based generic silhouette or a simple mountain glyph.\n\n---\n\nWant me to go ahead and implement one of these directions, or would you prefer to see mockup images first?",
